#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482620} :activity/id #uuid "5f5a0a27-74df-55fb-b257-c512fcb73cb8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051034 :topic/id #uuid "7c092bea-3614-5bca-b402-7742647e083b" :topic/math-academy-id 6399} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2414084d-978d-5852-9a75-b14f36d5dc64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.008" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481869}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481869} :answer-field/id #uuid "817219d2-4fac-5374-9c4a-6822ef9e7011" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4b679574-cdae-5555-900f-72dfd634fac3" :question/math-academy-id "q-334797" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $3.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.25$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.25)≈0.125.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.10,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.10)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.25$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.25}{0.10}=2.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $3,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{3}=(2.5)^{3}=15.625.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.10,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈\\frac{0.125}{15.625}=0.008.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈0.008.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "017a4cef-d60d-58c4-aa28-d0d444164350" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482624}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221975 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c42e845-2d37-5c62-8435-55cbffc5e702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462285} {:answer/id #uuid "336b0018-6868-5229-a732-914282396c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462286}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462285} :answer-field/id #uuid "74a27cbe-7a31-5696-93a4-64fa5ae5e5c9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462896} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5d80c5e-1df7-5ab6-a247-0eedf2a21825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462287} {:answer/id #uuid "bc61ed5b-b2b1-5bf5-89e2-98973109ac8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462288} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb95d659-7aa2-5922-b590-ce7333f86a7a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462897} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb8c5c2f-7a3c-5fc5-8d79-d95df07bb8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462289} {:answer/id #uuid "f7308828-7ddd-5c00-8a47-b3eec31e20ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462290}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186462289} :answer-field/id #uuid "b27c7694-a58b-5f51-a22b-60b4c92a4e89" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9f2ba467-6ea9-5201-b847-1a519dc1ed8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333576" :question/problem "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a first-order numerical method?\n- **I.** $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}$\n- **II.** $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3}$\n- **III.** $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{5}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}=6(Δx)^{2+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{2}=0.3(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $9(Δx)^{3}=9(Δx)^{2+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "054123fc-51ab-5f29-abe7-820e908c72d0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482628}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308709 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d2823fe-ac2e-533e-b8ef-d3d1dec21d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306488}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306488} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf20e996-1ea4-5bae-aecf-b59e8d34708e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6a7b9303-06ef-5983-bd7d-577c4a789987" :question/math-academy-id "q-334946" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $1.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.5$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.5)≈0.2.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.25,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.25)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.25.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.25}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $1,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{1+1}={2}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.25,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.25)≈\\frac{0.2}{4}=0.05.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "110acc7d-2f94-5af9-b727-c5d13e21f510"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d20c43b-aebe-57e6-9524-dcd0d1e5d276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305899} {:answer/id #uuid "4e918a44-c564-5fed-a0ad-e307dda2bb46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305917} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb35312-b7af-5bdf-9c23-68c4c07a3a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306191} {:answer/id #uuid "ba45bf27-6034-5a2b-b224-609af04300e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306888} {:answer/id #uuid "f1834450-0072-5bfe-83d8-37d9f92b8791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307368}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306888} :answer-field/id #uuid "346676e0-48f8-5066-ac65-2d01b43c9ccf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "07099fdd-1348-5e3a-a68e-3d7b94b8ddb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-333702" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08)=0.13.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.02,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.02)=0.002.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.08$ to $0.02.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.08}{0.02}=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={4}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.13$ to $0.002.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.13}{0.002}=65.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{p+1} & ≈65 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{4}⁡(65) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.011… \\\\ p & ≈2.011….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $2.$"} :step/id #uuid "24fbfd65-2e32-5d51-9949-234d1117d72d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482650}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b510a71-8c9b-527d-9bcb-f5fbb3d49293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396439} {:answer/id #uuid "fb33a210-7096-5874-a3f2-db5201cb5c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396440} {:answer/id #uuid "7357b661-3a8e-586f-b745-87bada056b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.281" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396441} {:answer/id #uuid "368eff5d-c023-5e5b-be1f-e7e458e23832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396442} {:answer/id #uuid "07ef22c7-7e79-5318-91fd-2a287e7cf0ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.550" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396443}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396440} :answer-field/id #uuid "d920cb15-c78d-5c51-9a36-297e1002feed" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396438} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af30b4d5-ae13-5e76-aef4-2c8e10d268d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.016" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396445} {:answer/id #uuid "0d4d56fd-96cd-5769-9c15-8ba42c5adc88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.281" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396446} {:answer/id #uuid "14a16a56-934d-5ec6-8e25-d2388421e668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396447} {:answer/id #uuid "5a6cdaa2-9b9d-5112-9911-3369aa53afb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396448} {:answer/id #uuid "a158f595-cfa4-5af1-86e9-3373c27d36fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.550" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396449}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396446} :answer-field/id #uuid "22993398-663b-5a03-abf5-707daa93f877" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396444} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6e06075-4e2d-569c-b93f-165663c39144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "99.068" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396451} {:answer/id #uuid "f089d8dd-6f47-565b-ab6c-d6ae32100108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "102.385" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396452} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8aca3b-0007-5700-a38f-b489a38acf4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120.050" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396453} {:answer/id #uuid "ed2c50b5-edcd-59fc-87f9-4fe83145da6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "107.403" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396454} {:answer/id #uuid "b3fcccd7-bafd-5c90-975c-73cad92fbe95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "104.632" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396455}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396455} :answer-field/id #uuid "b053a512-93f4-5783-be56-305d82bb8d4b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396450} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85b7c966-24c0-5f9e-ba19-26d61189aa8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396457} {:answer/id #uuid "b09802b2-85c8-57e5-8e51-14a3469d9ee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396458} {:answer/id #uuid "7a131701-833d-535b-800c-3887b7fc4a46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396459} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d8158d-f371-5bb8-9048-14d8a5c82403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396460} {:answer/id #uuid "48d8e74d-7332-5d6c-91a3-aaeed8c4c07d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396461}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396460} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a5e6e5a-102a-53f8-be70-6fec86c3cf6a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2c78fbb9-dc1c-5d53-be56-9ae880822440" :question/math-academy-id "q-334724" :question/problem "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=8(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.5$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.5) & =8(0.5)^{5}+2(0.5)^{6} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 0.031\\,25+2\\cdot 0.015\\,625 \\\\  & =0.281\\,25 \\\\  & ≈0.281.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.2) & =8(0.2)^{5}+2(0.2)^{6} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 0.000\\,32+2\\cdot 0.000\\,064 \\\\  & =0.002\\,688 \\\\  & ≈0.003.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.2}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2.5}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.281\\,25$ to $0.002\\,688.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.281\\,25}{0.002\\,688}=104.631\\,69…≈104.632.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2.5}^{p+1} & ≈104.631\\,69… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(104.631\\,69…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈5.075… \\\\ p & ≈4.075….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=8(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{5})=O\\,((Δx)^{4+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$"} :step/id #uuid "d1344963-f111-577e-9b39-d9ba8d2a6219" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482618}}] :activity/title "Order of Numerical Methods" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481769}]}
